// A generic native cannot be used as a value: there is one C function behind it and no per-`T`
// instantiation to hand over, so the message says to wrap it in a lambda rather than reporting a
// type mismatch.
//
// This used `slice<int>` until 2026-10-18, when `slice` became a builtin to take either world --
// and builtins have their *own* refusal for value position, which is
// `function_values_builtin_rejected`'s subject. It then used `mutable_array_get<int>` until F209
// deleted that declaration, `b[i]` having stopped being a rewrite onto a call. `mutable_array_filled`
// is a generic native for the same reason both of those were, and is a single declaration rather
// than a member of an overload set, which would refuse value position for its own reason.
fn main() {
    val f = mutable_array_filled<int>
    print(f(3, 0))
}
